PythonObject // PythonObject -- floor division of Python objects
****************************************************************

  * Operator: *note //: (Macaulay2Doc)//,
  * Usage: 
        x // y
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note python object: PythonObject,, 
      * y, a *note python object: PythonObject,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note python object: PythonObject,, the floor quotient of x and y; 

Description
===========

Divide two Python objects, giving the "floor" quotient, e.g., when dividing two
integers, the result is the quotient using Euclidean division.

+------------------------------+
|i1 : toPython 5 // toPython 3 |
|                              |
|o1 = 1                        |
|                              |
|o1 : PythonObject of class int|
+------------------------------+

If one of the arguments is a Macaulay2 object, then it is first converted to a
Python object before dividing.

+------------------------------+
|i2 : toPython 9 // 5          |
|                              |
|o2 = 1                        |
|                              |
|o2 : PythonObject of class int|
+------------------------------+
|i3 : 11 // toPython 7         |
|                              |
|o3 = 1                        |
|                              |
|o3 : PythonObject of class int|
+------------------------------+

Note that the behavior of floor division for integers differs between Macaulay2
and Python.  In particular, in Macaulay2, the quotient is rounded down when y
is positive and up when y is negative.  In Python, the quotient is always
rounded down.

+------------------------------+
|i4 : 5 // -3                  |
|                              |
|o4 = -1                       |
+------------------------------+
|i5 : toPython 5 // -3         |
|                              |
|o5 = -2                       |
|                              |
|o5 : PythonObject of class int|
+------------------------------+

