PythonObject ^ PythonObject -- exponentiation of Python objects
***************************************************************

  * Operator: *note ^: (Macaulay2Doc)^,
  * Usage: 
        x ^ y
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note python object: PythonObject,, 
      * y, a *note python object: PythonObject,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note python object: PythonObject,, x raised to the y power; 

Description
===========

Raise one Python object to the power of another.

+------------------------------+
|i1 : (toPython 2)^(toPython 3)|
|                              |
|o1 = 8                        |
|                              |
|o1 : PythonObject of class int|
+------------------------------+

In addition to the Macaulay2-style ^, this operation is also available using
the Python-style **.

+------------------------------+
|i2 : toPython 2 ** toPython 3 |
|                              |
|o2 = 8                        |
|                              |
|o2 : PythonObject of class int|
+------------------------------+

Note that parentheses were not necessary in the latter case since ** has much
lower precedence than ^.

If one of the arguments is a Macaulay2 object, then it is first converted to a
Python object before exponentiating.

+------------------------------+
|i3 : (toPython 2)^3           |
|                              |
|o3 = 8                        |
|                              |
|o3 : PythonObject of class int|
+------------------------------+
|i4 : 4^(toPython 5)           |
|                              |
|o4 = 1024                     |
|                              |
|o4 : PythonObject of class int|
+------------------------------+

Caveat
======

In Python, the ^ operator corresponds to the bitwise xor operation. We use ^^
in this case for consistency with the rest of Macaulay2.

See also
========

  * *note PythonObject ^^ PythonObject: PythonObject ^^ PythonObject, --
    bitwise exclusive disjunction of Python objects
  * *note PythonObject ^= Thing: PythonObject ^= Thing, -- augmented
    exponentiation for Python objects
* Menu:

* PythonObject ^= Thing::       augmented exponentiation for Python objects

