PythonObject and PythonObject -- logical conjunction of Python objects
**********************************************************************

  * Operator: *note and: (Macaulay2Doc)and,
  * Usage: 
        x and y
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note python object: PythonObject,, 
      * y, a *note python object: PythonObject,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note python object: PythonObject,, logical conjunction of x and y; 

Description
===========

Perform the logical conjunction ("and") operation on Python objects.

+------------------------------------+
|i1 : toPython true and toPython true|
|                                    |
|o1 = True                           |
|                                    |
|o1 : PythonObject of class bool     |
+------------------------------------+

If one of the arguments is a Macaulay2 object, then it is first converted to a
Python object.

+----------------------------+
|i2 : toPython true and false|
|                            |
|o2 = false                  |
+----------------------------+
|i3 : false and toPython true|
|                            |
|o3 = false                  |
+----------------------------+

Unlike Macaulay2, Python supports using and with non-boolean objects.  In this
case, the first argument is returned if it is "falsy". Otherwise, the second
argument is returned.

+----------------------------------+
|i4 : toPython 0 and toPython "foo"|
|                                  |
|o4 = 0                            |
|                                  |
|o4 : PythonObject of class int    |
+----------------------------------+
|i5 : toPython 5 and toPython "bar"|
|                                  |
|o5 = bar                          |
|                                  |
|o5 : PythonObject of class str    |
+----------------------------------+

In Python, when the first argument is falsy, then is is returned immediately
without evaluating the second argument.  This is known as "short-circuiting".
However, in Macaulay2, both arguments are evaluated before the Python
conjunction method is called.

+-------------------------------------------+
|i6 : stopIfError = false                   |
|                                           |
|o6 = false                                 |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i7 : toPython 0 and 1/0                    |
|stdio:7:16:(3):[1]: error: division by zero|
+-------------------------------------------+

However, if the first argument is the Macaulay2 *note false:
(Macaulay2Doc)false, object, then short-cicuiting will occur.

+--------------------------------+
|i8 : false and pythonValue "1/0"|
|                                |
|o8 = false                      |
+--------------------------------+

